<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volksraad (Dutch East Indies)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Volksraad (People's Council) was an advisory and later partly legislative assembly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, which first met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> in 1918. Its members were partly elected by local councils and partly appointed, and included Indonesians, Europeans, and Chinese and Arab residents. It gave Indonesian nationalists a public forum but had little real power, and it ceased to function after the Japanese invasion of 1942.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volksraad-dutch-east-indies.jpg" alt="Volksraad (Dutch East Indies)" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:State_coat_of_arms_of_the_Netherlands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea of a representative body grew partly out of the Dutch Ethical Policy. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Dutch feared a Japanese threat and asked Indonesian leaders whether Indonesians would serve in a militia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budi-utomo">Budi Utomo</a> supported the idea, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarekat-islam">Sarekat Islam</a> refused to fight for a colony in which it had no legislative voice, and Budi Utomo came round to that view by 1915. In 1916–1917 delegates of these groups and the Union of Regents toured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and pleaded their case with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmina-of-the-netherlands">Queen Wilhelmina</a>.</p><p>On 16 December 1916, on a proposal of the Colonial Affairs Minister Thomas Bastiaan Pleyte, a law established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volksraad">Volksraad</a> as an advisory body without legislative powers. Governor-General Johan Paul, Count van Limburg Stirum, founded it at Weltevreden on 18 May 1918, in a building on Jalan Pejambon now known as the Pancasila Building.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In its first session the council rejected a loyal cable to the queen and allowed debates in Malay. In November 1918 the Radicale Concentratie faction, led by Tjokroaminoto, called for an elected parliament with the right to pass laws, and the governor-general promised extensive reforms. A commission under Professor Carpentier Alting studied the motion, which was rejected in 1920; the unkept pledges became known as the November promises.</p><p>In 1927 the Volksraad became a co-legislative body, though the governor-general kept a veto, and Mohammad Husni Thamrin founded a National Faction aiming at full independence. On 15 July 1936 members led by Soetardjo Kartohadikusumo submitted a petition for autonomy within a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> commonwealth, which passed by 26 votes to 20 once its ten-year deadline was dropped; the Dutch government rejected it on 16 November 1938. The Visman Commission's report of 1942 granted only the use of the word Indonesian instead of Native, and after the Japanese occupation began the Dutch dissolved the council.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The council began with 39 members, 15 of them native-born Indonesians and the rest Europeans and so-called foreign orientals; from 1927 it had 60 seats, of which 30 were held by native-born members, 20 elected and 10 appointed. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> constitution was revised in 1922 the Dutch, Indonesians and other orientals elected their members separately, which kept the Indonesians from a majority. In the last election, in 1939, one tenth of the population chose 937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> and a further 515 were appointed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Volksraad (Dutch East Indies)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A <b>People’s Council</b> (Volksraad), first meeting in 1918, gave Indonesians a limited advisory voice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The constitution of 1854, which drew partly on the constitution of the United States, created a single-chamber <b>Volksraad</b> elected by the burghers and a president elected directly for a five-year term, assisted by an executive council.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Very little is recorded beyond its small <b>Volksraad</b> and a population of a few dozen farming families.</blockquote><a href="/state/klein-vrystaat">Klein Vrystaat</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic set up a <b>Volksraad</b> and founded its capital at Vryheid.</blockquote><a href="/state/nieuwe-republiek">Nieuwe Republiek</a></li></ul></section>
